# 1D finite-element bar, 2000 elements, assembled algebraically (dogfood: structures).
#
# bar_fem.sky assembles K with a loop of 2x2 scatter-adds, written as
# K = K + Pᵀ ke P. Because arrays are immutable every iteration allocates and
# adds a whole n x n matrix, so assembly is O(n^3) work and at n = 2000 it
# takes ~10 s. The same matrix is K = k Bᵀ B, where B is the (n x n+1)
# difference (strain-displacement) operator, one Gram product `Bᵀ @ B`
# that Sky computes as a symmetric rank-k update. Here that is 50x faster, and
# gives bit-identical displacements. The catch: it only works when every
# element has the same stiffness (no per-element weights without diag()).

E = 200 GPa
A = 1 cm^2
L = 2 m
F = 1 kN

Le = L / 2000
k = E * A / Le
B = identity(2001)[0:2000, :] - identity(2001)[1:2001, :]   # strain = B u (up to 1/Le)
K = k * (Bᵀ @ B)                                            # 2001 x 2001, N/m
Kr = K[1:2001, 1:2001]                                      # clamp node 0
f = F * identity(2000)[1999]
u = Kr⁻¹ @ f

print("tip displacement:", u[1999] as mm)
print("exact F L/(E A): ", F * L / (E * A) as mm)

err = 0 m
for i in range(2000):
    err = max(err, abs(u[i] - F * ((i + 1) * Le) / (E * A)))
print("max nodal error: ", err)
assert err < 1e-9 m
